#{[17592186093264 #edb/ref 1061 464474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093264 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c3d8379-0f57-5843-bba4-5e8fe0677978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093264 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf R$ by its standard matrix $R,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{R}$ by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =R(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {j}^{'} & =R(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/35/3516ba56a842adb78f1acae9190b65eb1574629e9f3d6bca25854f547f69385f.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $30^\\circ$ about the origin.**\n\nAs usual, we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $360^\\circ-30^\\circ=330^\\circ$ *clockwise* about the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1065 3546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "427eca20-6aad-5c52-a545-fc66d808e5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093433 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093435 #edb/ref 1064 "The matrix $R_\\theta,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nrepresents a **rotation by an angle $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.**\n\nTo find the matrix representing a rotation by an angle $\\theta$ *clockwise* about the origin, we replace $\\theta$ with $-\\theta$ in the above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta ) & -\\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) & \\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186093435 #edb/ref 1065 3548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093435 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "333a7749-1cde-5720-8a91-4a43d26fc0fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093435 #edb/ref 1067 "General Rotation Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186112026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112021 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4b71e61e-8356-5139-84d3-9e36d53bf0df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112022 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186