 [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1061 17221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e9e3e32-4737-52d8-8430-910568cdf491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116125 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1061 17223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5bdf1c7-f166-5b10-9f1a-0aab443b2ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116126 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1061 17224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9582140d-32a5-56c0-81bf-8a788ac9965a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116127 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1061 17225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "842b6803-5ec6-5291-b4c6-ea03cd8e381f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116144 #edb/ref 1061 657175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "418c7a1f-9223-51fd-97f4-1803f5d7ca05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116145 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116145 #edb/ref 1061 657176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116145 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c356c496-7618-5d3a-9305-29052fb2b027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116145 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116146 #edb/ref 1061 657182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116146 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ac70c4b-4bc1-52fe-a19b-eb181fb3e1b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116146 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116147 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116147 #edb/ref 1061 657179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d258ab02-0d7e-57e3-b5cb-8234c654f707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1064 "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix ![](images/09/09dfa5716dbd500a4684f62a5c409a392761e7546c7ac5e4b592133623552506.svg) we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n![](images/44/44bf82527f952ba4d01bbc16f0b81a47fe9e82f447a996f75bccef5171d92242.svg)\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1065 3164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "85735558-3e6a-5a6e-be0b-95227712e60a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116495 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1065 3165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24a632e9-4a94-5212-b848-8d4b4403d452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116496 #edb/ref 1067 "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116502 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find the inverse of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ using elementary row operations on the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[A\\, |\\, I],\n$$\n\n \nwhere $I$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix.\nWe do this by transforming the left-hand side of the augmented matrix into the identity matrix, which gives an augmented matrix of the form \n\n$$\n[ I\\, |\\, A^{-1}].\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, this method works for any invertible matrix!\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the inverse of the $4\\times 4$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/15/159ac4bad9c080651266ed6cc2369149c38c38dfb276b27e6c6dccea6d7601c9.svg)\n\nWe write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$, as shown below. Then, we apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n![](images/24/2470c145390237d588db20950bdf573f79961929bbf2051e8f4a067b977ddc68.svg)\n\nTherefore, the inverse matrix is\n![](images/64/6441521c48a9d1ba632eda037531749ee41454b7c616d5d88bf522a4b2dcd458.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116502 #edb/ref 1065 3172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116502 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "155c4e79-e15d-5e2b-88eb-14556fd8e7f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116502 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the LU Factorization of a 4x4 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a86c24c2-97c2-534e-9c03-9735b031bb23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243991 #edb/re