2186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b63f5657-355a-5f0a-a214-2065b7a76d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1061 20109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "babad562-3d88-57e5-a6c7-a8b53bec4956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1061 20111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46208d38-6fce-5121-8f5a-512e318a7731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1061 20113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4880d3b1-8971-5b5f-8646-d786ae6c89fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1061 31946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e492707b-f646-51d5-b4f4-34c984760081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1061 34230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "965fe16f-9c57-5058-9e32-90332314977c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1061 34231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd127c03-d025-5b21-ae93-e24b7aec916f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1061 34232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "429ea8df-33bc-5c79-a0d6-97b71166cd98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1061 34233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba24a30-9b4e-5e58-93b1-b999bb45fe16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1061 34234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3865f17c-09ee-5b73-96f9-45f1dd985dd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1061 34235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "735b7b96-43fa-5c0e-b4b8-df7598a6610b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1061 37332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c429a3d-9dd8-5fe2-a7e7-cf0e9f515d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1065 3569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e561ab8-38f8-5ec1-a285-6559f35aa7cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1065 5500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1067 "Steady-State Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1064 "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1065 5501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1064 "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n> *Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> converges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*\n\nIn other words, if [math]P[/math] is a regular [math]m \\times m[/math] stochastic matrix and [math]\\mathbf{q}_0 \\in \\mathbf{R}^m[/math] is an arbitrary (initial) stochastic vector, then [math]P[/math] has the unique steady state vector [math]\\mathbf{q}[/math] and \n\n\\[\nP^n \\mathbf{q}_0 \\to \\mathbf{q} \n\\]\n\nas [math]n \\to \\infty.[/math]" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1065 5502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1067 "Regular Stochastic Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1065 6151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186480634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129130 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4e